From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] linker: search the VNDK APEX when there is no ld.config.txt

A Halium GSI ships no /system/etc/ld.config.txt, so every process falls back
to init_default_namespace_no_config() and gets kDefaultLdPaths: /system/lib64,
/odm/lib64 and /vendor/lib64. That covered everything until Android 11 moved
the VNDK into an APEX.

A stock Android 11 vendor expects the VNDK to be reachable. On a Pixel 3a the
audio HAL links android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so, which is shipped
nowhere but /apex/com.android.vndk.v30/lib64, so loading it fails with

    library "android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so" not found
    E droid-util.c: Failed to load audio hw module audio.primary : Invalid argument (22)

and pulseaudio dies on module-droid-card. Stock Android reaches that directory
through the "vndk" namespace ld.config.txt declares and links the vendor
namespace to; with no config file there is no such namespace and no link.

Scan /apex instead of hardcoding a level - the directory is named for the
vendor's VNDK version, which differs per device and per Android release - and
append the result after the built-in paths so /system, /odm and /vendor still
take precedence.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
--- a/common/q/linker.cpp
+++ b/common/q/linker.cpp
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <android/api-level.h>
+#include <dirent.h>
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <inttypes.h>
@@ -4154,6 +4155,45 @@
     }
   }
 
+  // Add whatever VNDK APEX is mounted.
+  //
+  // A Halium GSI ships no /system/etc/ld.config.txt, so this function -- not
+  // the configured path below -- is what every process ends up with, and
+  // kDefaultLdPaths only knows /system, /odm and /vendor. That was enough
+  // until Android 11 moved the VNDK into an APEX. A stock Android 11 vendor's
+  // libraries expect to find it there: the Pixel 3a's audio HAL links
+  // android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so, which exists nowhere but
+  // /apex/com.android.vndk.v30/lib64, so it fails to load with
+  //
+  //     library "android.hardware.power-V1-ndk_platform.so" not found
+  //
+  // and every consumer of that HAL dies with it. Stock Android reaches the
+  // same directory through the "vndk" namespace that ld.config.txt sets up;
+  // with no config there is no such namespace and no link to it.
+  //
+  // Scan /apex rather than hardcoding a level: the directory is named for the
+  // vendor's VNDK version, which differs per device and per Android release.
+  // Appended after the built-in paths, so /system, /odm and /vendor still win.
+  DIR* apex_dir = opendir("/apex");
+  if (apex_dir != nullptr) {
+    static const char kVndkPrefix[] = "com.android.vndk.v";
+    for (dirent* entry = readdir(apex_dir); entry != nullptr; entry = readdir(apex_dir)) {
+      if (strncmp(entry->d_name, kVndkPrefix, sizeof(kVndkPrefix) - 1) != 0) {
+        continue;
+      }
+      std::string vndk_lib_dir = std::string("/apex/") + entry->d_name +
+#if defined(__LP64__)
+                                 "/lib64";
+#else
+                                 "/lib";
+#endif
+      if (realpath(vndk_lib_dir.c_str(), real_path) != nullptr) {
+        ld_default_paths.push_back(real_path);
+      }
+    }
+    closedir(apex_dir);
+  }
+
   g_default_namespace->set_default_library_paths(std::move(ld_default_paths));
 
   std::vector<android_namespace_t*> namespaces;
